1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,472
The Curiosity Club.

2
00:00:02,472 --> 00:00:05,067
Episode 36.

3
00:00:05,067 --> 00:00:07,663
A Message Made of Flashes.

4
00:00:07,663 --> 00:00:10,557
Here is something to listen for.

5
00:00:10,557 --> 00:00:15,149
Why did the first three flashes fail to communicate Maya's meaning?

6
00:00:15,149 --> 00:00:16,671
Ready?

7
00:00:16,671 --> 00:00:18,777
Let us find out.

8
00:00:18,777 --> 00:00:22,608
The classroom is divided into two pretend islands.

9
00:00:22,608 --> 00:00:26,399
Maya stands on one side with a small flashlight.

10
00:00:26,399 --> 00:00:29,687
Leo stands on the other with a notebook.

11
00:00:29,687 --> 00:00:33,410
No shouting across the ocean, Ms. Park says.

12
00:00:33,410 --> 00:00:36,807
Your challenge is to send a message with light.

13
00:00:36,807 --> 00:00:39,483
Maya flashes three times.

14
00:00:39,483 --> 00:00:42,866
Leo writes: Three snacks, please.

15
00:00:42,866 --> 00:00:45,869
That is not what I meant, she says.

16
00:00:45,869 --> 00:00:48,967
Pip writes: Three robots arriving.

17
00:00:48,967 --> 00:00:50,869
Also wrong.

18
00:00:50,869 --> 00:00:54,008
They realize they need an agreed code.

19
00:00:54,008 --> 00:00:58,410
They choose one short flash for yes and two short flashes for no.

20
00:00:58,410 --> 00:01:01,589
A longer pause separates messages.

21
00:01:01,589 --> 00:01:04,565
Now Ms. Park asks questions.

22
00:01:04,565 --> 00:01:06,983
Is the library open?

23
00:01:06,983 --> 00:01:08,967
One flash.

24
00:01:08,967 --> 00:01:11,059
Is Pip a penguin?

25
00:01:11,059 --> 00:01:13,029
Two flashes.

26
00:01:13,029 --> 00:01:17,894
Pip requests a third answer: Occasionally, during dance practice.

27
00:01:17,894 --> 00:01:21,345
The children later try a simple letter chart.

28
00:01:21,345 --> 00:01:27,228
They discover that sending letters takes patience and that unclear pauses can change the message.

29
00:01:27,228 --> 00:01:35,774
Ms. Park explains that people have used many kinds of signals to carry information, including light, sound, and electrical pulses.

30
00:01:35,774 --> 00:01:38,967
A code connects patterns with meanings.

31
00:01:38,967 --> 00:01:42,744
Leo deliberately sends one unclear pattern.

32
00:01:42,744 --> 00:01:46,413
Maya asks him to repeat it instead of guessing.

33
00:01:46,413 --> 00:01:50,122
That is good communication, Ms. Park says.

34
00:01:50,122 --> 00:01:53,532
Checking a message can be part of the system.

35
00:01:53,532 --> 00:01:58,953
Pip sends two careful flashes and then holds up a sign: No penguins detected.

36
00:01:58,953 --> 00:02:01,277
Leo looks relieved.

37
00:02:01,277 --> 00:02:04,728
The classroom ocean is safe for another day.

38
00:02:04,728 --> 00:02:07,744
Now, a little more to discover.

39
00:02:07,744 --> 00:02:13,586
A signal can carry information when sender and receiver share a rule for interpreting it.

40
00:02:13,586 --> 00:02:16,535
Codes assign meanings to patterns.

41
00:02:16,535 --> 00:02:21,413
Timing and separation can matter as much as the individual signals.

42
00:02:21,413 --> 00:02:27,894
Real communication systems also need ways to handle errors or ask for information to be sent again.

43
00:02:27,894 --> 00:02:30,923
Let us hear the main idea again.

44
00:02:30,923 --> 00:02:34,361
Random flashes produced different guesses.

45
00:02:34,361 --> 00:02:39,538
Once the children agreed on a code and pauses, they could exchange messages.

46
00:02:39,538 --> 00:02:45,135
When a pattern was unclear, asking for a repeat worked better than inventing an answer.

47
00:02:45,135 --> 00:02:48,138
Here are three useful expressions.

48
00:02:48,138 --> 00:02:51,182
You can listen, or say them with me.

49
00:02:51,182 --> 00:02:53,654
What does this signal mean?

50
00:02:53,654 --> 00:02:56,127
What does this signal mean?

51
00:02:56,127 --> 00:02:58,451
Could you send that again?

52
00:02:58,451 --> 00:03:00,774
Could you send that again?

53
00:03:00,774 --> 00:03:03,559
We need the same set of rules.

54
00:03:03,559 --> 00:03:06,345
We need the same set of rules.

55
00:03:06,345 --> 00:03:09,211
Remember our listening question?

56
00:03:09,211 --> 00:03:13,804
Why did the first three flashes fail to communicate Maya's meaning?

57
00:03:13,804 --> 00:03:15,991
Here is the answer.

58
00:03:15,991 --> 00:03:21,752
The group had not agreed on a code, so each person gave the flashes a different meaning.

59
00:03:21,752 --> 00:03:25,543
And here is a question just for your imagination.

60
00:03:25,543 --> 00:03:30,774
What simple signal could your family use to mean that someone needs a quiet moment?

61
00:03:30,774 --> 00:03:33,940
There is more than one possible answer.

62
00:03:33,940 --> 00:03:37,092
Keep wondering, and see you next time.
